Combat "Juice" & Kinesthetic Impact:
Hades feels incredible because of three specific micro-feel mechanics:
Wall-Slam Physics: When you knock an enemy into a pillar, wall, or barrier, they bounce off it, take extra crush damage, crack the masonry, and trigger a mini screen-shake. Right now, our enemies just slide back.
Directional Flinch & Weight: When Zagreus swings a heavy blade, the enemy's entire body recoils in the exact vector of the blade swing before recovering. Currently, enemies flash and take damage, but their physical reaction doesn't have that visceral crunch.
Micro-Hitstop Stagger: A 4-frame pause on the exact frame of weapon contact, giving your brain that tactile sensation of blade cutting through armor.
Audio Landscape:
Darren Korb’s sound design in Hades is 40% of why people love the game — crunching bone impacts, crisp blade rings, heavy bass drops when a room clears, and dynamic acoustic-metal riffs that build as more enemies spawn.
Our Web Audio synth engine handles combat sounds well without needing 500MB of external audio files, but synthesized SFX will never match studio-recorded foley and live guitars.
