If I am 100% honest with you as your lead engineer: No, a browser WebGL/Three.js engine is not equal to native Unreal Engine 5 in raw 3D graphics power—and anyone who tells you a browser engine can match Black Myth: Wukong’s native UE5 rendering 1-to-1 is lying to you.
Here is the real, unfiltered breakdown of where our Meru Engine v2.0 actually stands compared to Unreal Engine 5, why it looks the way it does right now, and what it actually takes to reach true AAA visual parity.
1. Where Unreal Engine 5 Is in Another League (Native GPU Hardware)
Unreal Engine 5 runs directly on native DirectX 12 / Vulkan with 15–50 GB of local disk assets and full GPU compute shaders. That gives UE5 three things a browser tab cannot replicate natively:
Nanite (Virtualized Micropolygon Geometry): UE5 can render 20 million+ triangles per frame (every crack in a stone temple pillar, every link of chainmail on Vrikodara) with zero LOD pop-in. In a browser, downloading a 500 MB mesh would freeze the page load, so web models have to stay around 20,000–80,000 triangles and rely on 2K Normal Maps for surface detail.
Hardware Ray-Traced Lumen: UE5 traces real 3D light rays bouncing off every polygon in real time. In Meru Engine v2.0, we use PMREM Image-Based Lighting (IBL) for 3D metallic reflections plus 2.5D radial screen-space GI light pools—which looks great from a fixed isometric angle, but is an approximation, not true ray-tracing.
Full-Scene 3D Deferred Shading: In UE5, the floor, the pillars, the volumetric fire, the characters, and the bosses all live inside one unified 3D depth buffer, casting real 3D shadows onto each other.
2. Why Our Game Still Feels "Browser / Indie" in Spots Right Now (The Real Bottleneck)
Even with the UE5-style PBR, ACES Filmic tone mapping, IBL metallic reflections, and post-processing we just added, there is one architectural reason our vertical slice still doesn’t look like a unified AAA release yet:
Right now, our arena is a "Mixed-Media Composite" instead of a Unified World:
The Floor: A flat 2D painted image (HTMLCanvasElement drawImage).
Rudra, Dundubhi & Vrikodara: Real 3D WebGL .fbx models rendered on offscreen 384×384 Three.js canvases and stamped onto the 2D arena.
Regular Enemies (Stalkers, Archers, Golems): 4-frame 2D sprite sheets (4x1 PNG strips) that slide and flip horizontally.
Because flat 2D painted floors + 4-frame 2D sprite enemies + 3D FBX bosses are all sitting next to each other on a 2D canvas:
A 4-frame 2D enemy sprite only has 4 poses total (so it looks stiff compared to a 60 FPS 3D character or a 60-frame Hades II sprite).
The 3D characters don't cast real 3D shadows onto the 3D geometry of the temple pillars—they cast oval 2D contact shadows onto a flat background picture.
3. Can Our Browser Engine Match Hades II Visually? YES. Can It Match Black Myth: Wukong? NO.
It comes down to which camera and art direction we commit to:
Visual Target	Can Meru Engine (Browser) Match It?	Why / What’s Missing Right Now
Black Myth: Wukong / God of War (Photorealistic 3D over-the-shoulder)	No (Impossible in Browser)	Requires UE5 Nanite, 40+ GB of 8K scans, hardware ray-tracing, and full-body motion capture.
Hades II / No Rest for the Wicked / Diablo IV (Stylized Isometric 3D/2.5D)	Yes (85–90% Achievable in Browser)	In an isometric camera, the camera is far enough back that lighting, silhouette, animation fluidity, and unified 3D/PBR rendering matter much more than 20M-polygon Nanite meshes.
